# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Planning and session notes — kept on disk, deliberately not published.
#
# These are the working notes the project was built from: a backlog, a
# session handoff, deployment and cost notes, and the per-phase specs and
# implementation plans under docs/superpowers/. They are genuinely useful
# locally and are still written and read every session — they are ignored,
# not deleted. Owner's call (2026-08-06): the repo should carry the code,
# not the process behind it.
#
# Safe to ignore because nothing in src/ or tests/ references a docs/ path.
# Code comments cite spec *sections* ("spec §6:108", "3c D3") rather than
# file paths, so none of them dangle. Keep it that way: if a comment ever
# needs to point at a document, quote the reasoning inline instead.
#
# NOT ignored, on purpose:
#   AGENTS.md   — the "this Next.js version has breaking changes, read
#                 node_modules/next/dist/docs/ first" warning. Five lines,
#                 and the one thing a newcomer must read before writing any
#                 Next code in this repo.
#   CLAUDE.md   — one line, includes AGENTS.md.
#   README.md   — the repo's front door.
#   docs/security/ — the npm-audit triage explains why 12 high findings are
#                 not production-reachable. That is about the dependencies,
#                 not about the process, and it saves the next person the
#                 same investigation.
/TODO.md
/HANDOFF.md
/TOMORROW.md
/DEPLOY.md
/docs/superpowers/
/docs/design/claude-design-prompt.md
.superpowers/
